/// A [`States`] type or tuple of types which implement [`States`].
///
/// This trait is used allow implementors of [`States`], as well
```

If we proceed with the suggestions in [this comment](https://github.com/bevyengine/bevy/pull/11426#issuecomment-1913275021), I'd suggest the computed state for InGame and TurboMode follow that of `IsPaused`.

I.e.:
```
enum IsInGame {
    NotInGame,
    InGame,
}

enum IsTurbo {
    NotTurbo,
    Turbo,
}
```

I think that most accurately represents the purpose of what these computed states represent: states with two variants derived from a more complex root state. This would also increase the consistency across the example and remove the need for reasoning about optional or removed states.

I think having data in the InGame struct is more idiomatic when trying to do enum variant types (which is kind of what we're doing here).

```rust
enum AppState {
    #[default]
    Menu,
    InGame(InGame),
}

struct InGame {
    paused: bool,
    turbo: bool,
}
```

Can we address sub-states here?
It's common for them to not exist at given point in time and setting their `NextState<S>` should be well defined.

IMO it should be cleared if the sub-state doesn't exist.
A reactive resource like `NextState` would be weird if it persisted, acted with delay.

It should also be the initial value of a sub-state when it comes to exist.
E.g.
```rs
enum AppState {
  Menu,
  Game,
}

enum GameState {
  Tutorial,
  Campaign,
  Custom,
}

fn main() {
  // [...]
  app.init_state::<AppState>()
        .add_sub_state::<GameState>();
}

fn menu_ui(app: ResMut<NextState<AppState>>, game: ResMut<NextState<GameState>>) {
  // [...]
  app.set(AppState::Game); // Change root state
  match mode { // Change sub-state when it comes to exist
    "tutorial" => game.set(GameState::Tutorial),
    "campaign" => game.set(GameState::Campaign),
    "custom" => game.set(GameState::Custom),
  }
}
```

For the scope of this PR and your sanity, I think we should decide on the correct scenario and ensure it's handled in another PR.
